WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:23.200
If you are a film buff, you should be familiar
with the early Soviet movie Battleship Potemkin

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:28.860
and its iconic scene of a pram rolling down
a staircase, as soldiers shoot down protesters.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:34.160
The film is centred around the Russian Revolution
of 1905 and directed by cinematic legend Sergej

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.160
Eisenstein.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.370
Eisenstein would later direct ‘October’,
a retelling of that other uprising -- the

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:44.310
successful one -- the Russian Revolution of
1917.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:50.059
This film features our protagonist of today,
Alexander Kerensky, depicted as a pompous,

00:00:50.059 --> 00:00:54.870
poor man’s Napoleon, his image juxtaposed
with that of a mechanical peacock.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.829
Kerensky was portrayed as a clear symbol of
artificial vanity.

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:03.910
This unflattering portrayal reflects the Bolsheviks’
opinion of Kerensky, who in modern times has

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:08.720
been relegated to a mere footnote in the early
history of the Soviet Union.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:14.430
Yet despite the Bolshevik’s perception and
Eisenstein’s clever montage techniques,

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:17.030
Alexander Kerensky was a revolutionary.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.979
He had a crucial role in the overthrow of
the Tsars.

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:25.800
He had been a central figure in the earlier
revolution of February 1917, when he had led

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.540
the Provisional Government and sought to install
a democracy in Russia.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.310
Did he deserve this hollow legacy?

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:41.390
Let’s find out in today’s Biographic about
Alexander Kerensky, the early revolutionary.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:49.640
A Dancing Lawyer
Alexander Kerensky was born on May 4, 1881

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.220
in the town of Simbirsk.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:55.830
This town is now known as Ul’ianovsk, after
another famous fellow who was born there 11

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.880
years earlier: Vladimir Ilic Ulianov, better
known as Lenin.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.200
Alexander’s father was a teacher and director
of the local gymnasium, the same where Lenin

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.200
had studied.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.130
The Kerenskys and the Ulianovs in fact were
on friendly terms, and belonged to the same

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.140
middle class intellectual circle.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:15.480
In 1899, Alexander completed his studies at
the gymnasium, graduating with a gold medal

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.480
for distinction.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.720
He had the reputation of being a polite, smart
young man.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:23.880
More importantly, he was a skilled dancer
and even a gifted actor.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:29.470
After high school, Kerensky enrolled at the
Law faculty of St Petersburg University, graduating

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:30.470
in 1904.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:35.250
During the same year, he married the daughter
of a General, Olga Baranovskaia, whom I am

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:38.440
sure he seduced with his smooth dance moves.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.740
Alexander and Olga later had two sons.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.950
1905 was the year that changed everything
for the young Kerensky.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:49.200
Sunday the 22nd of January became known as
Bloody Sunday – one of the too many Bloody

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.020
Sundays of human history.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:55.340
Russian society was gripped in unrest, due
to the unpopularity of the Government and

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.530
the debacle of the ongoing Russo-Japanese
War.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:02.550
Crowds had gathered for a peaceful demonstration
outside the Winter Palace in St Petersburg,

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:07.700
when the army and the police opened fire,
killing and injuring around 1,000 people.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:12.260
The Revolution of 1905 continued with the
mutiny of the battleship Potemkin in June,

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.080
which inspired the film I mentioned earlier.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.610
The resulting riots were quashed by troops
on the Tsar’s orders.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:23.870
On the 30th of October, Tsar Nicholas II issued
the October Manifesto, which promised limited

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:27.710
civil liberties and an elected parliament,
the Duma.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:31.010
In the same month, Kerensky had begun his
career as a political lawyer.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:35.850
At a trial in Tallinn -- present-day Estonia
-- he defended the peasants who had ransacked

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.140
a local aristocrat’s mansion.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:43.080
Kerensky continued to defend revolutionary
activists in court and joined the Aid Committee

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.960
for the victims of Bloody Sunday.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.870
This revolutionary fervour was made clear
when he joined the Socialist Revolutionary

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:54.130
Party , becoming the editor of their newspaper,
Burevestnik, or The Thunderbird.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:55.880
The Thunderbird was go!

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:00.040
Under Kerensky’s management, it increased
its circulation, but attracted the attention

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.400
of the authorities along the way.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.959
The newspaper became the object of police
repression: the eighth issue was confiscated,

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:10.690
and on the 21st of December, Kerensky’s
flat was searched.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:15.770
The police found incriminating evidence -- leaflets
advertising the “Armed Uprising Organization,”

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:18.430
which was considered to be a terrorist outfit.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:21.230
Kerensky was held in custody until April 1906.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:26.300
He was released due to a lack of evidence,
but the Tsarist police sent him on a ‘forced

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:30.590
vacation’ to Tashkent, Uzbekistan, together
with his wife Olga and their one-year-old

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:31.840
son Oleg.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.840
In autumn 1906 he was allowed to return to
St. Petersburg, where he resumed his activity

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.870
as a popular lawyer, famous for his oratory
skills at political trials.

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:47.040
In 1910, Kerensky was the chief defender of
the Turkestan social revolutionary organization,

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.680
accused of staging armed, anti-government
acts.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.510
The trial went well for the defendants, as
the young lawyer succeeded in preventing any

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:54.639
death sentences.

00:04:54.639 --> 00:05:00.180
In 1912, he also shined as the defender of
a group of Dashnaks, Armenian activists, accused

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:02.790
of terrorist acts.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:09.780
Storm Clouds Gather
These trials vaulted Kerensky into the political

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.090
stage of his era.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:17.020
He grew in status, and in 1912 he was elected
Deputy of the Fourth State Duma from the Trudoviki,

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:18.020
or “Labour Group.”

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:23.520
He soon became the faction’s leader and
joined a freemason society to unite the anti-Tsarist

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.340
forces striving for the democratic renewal
of Russia.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:31.370
His popularity grew even more when he exposed
Roman Malinovsky, an early Bolshevik leader,

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:35.350
as an undercover agent of the Okhrana, the
Tsar’s secret police.

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:40.220
In Kerensky, the young Labour movement had
found an effective leader, a vocal critic

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.270
of the Government, and one of the best speakers
on the left.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:49.190
By the time World War I broke out in August
1914, Kerenski was the Duma's highest profile

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:54.140
radical, as well as one of the few to oppose
Russia's entrance in the conflict.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:59.250
But in late 1915, Kerensky adopted a lower
profile at a Duma, as his health had declined

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:00.670
because of a kidney disease.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:06.250
His condition continued to deteriorate, and
in April 1916, Kerensky had to go to Sweden

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:07.790
to have a kidney removed.

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:12.140
According to his grandson Stephen, he was
almost constantly on intravenous morphine

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:13.830
for the rest of his life.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:18.830
Kerensky returned to the capital in the summer,
which had now been re-christened as Petrograd.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:20.680
His stance on the war had changed.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:26.660
He was now fully committed to Russia’s participation
in the war, as a means to oppose German militarism.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:31.120
None of that could get Nicholas II off the
hook, though, as Kerensky continued his personal

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:32.660
attacks on the Tsar.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:37.250
The Monarch had taken personal command of
the Armed Forces in September 1915 – something

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:39.240
he should have left to the professionals.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.470
Now, everybody was against him and his poor
command skills.

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:49.750
By the end of 1916, Kerensky was publicly
calling for the Tsar’s abdication.

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:57.330
Revolution and Bad Moves
In February of 1917, Kerensky left Labour

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:00.330
to rejoin the Socialist Revolutionary Party.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:06.100
He called again for the removal of Nicholas
II, which prompted Tsarina Alexandra to demand

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:07.510
that he be hanged as a traitor.

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:09.320
The royal family caved first.

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:13.070
The pressure was too much, and the Tsar abdicated
on March 13.

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:17.710
A Provisional Government was formed under
Prince George Lvov, who appointed Kerensky

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:19.290
as Minister of Justice.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:24.479
The cabinet was now ruled by the Constitutional
Democrats or ‘Cadet’ party, in a tense

00:07:24.479 --> 00:07:26.740
alliance with the more radical left.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:31.710
The energic Alexander immediately abolished
capital punishment and introduced other reforms:

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:36.120
freedom of the press, the abolition of ethnic
and religious discrimination and even set

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:38.490
plans for universal suffrage.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:42.460
The Provisional Government had no intention
to quit the war at this stage.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:47.360
Foreign Minister Milyukov sent a note to the
Entente to inform them of this policy.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.729
When the note became public ... the Bolsheviks,
the Soviets of the soldiers and tens of millions

00:07:51.729 --> 00:07:55.430
of Russians were not amused, to put it mildly.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:08:00.120
That disastrous war had rallied the people
against the Tsar, and now the Provisional

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:02.400
Government wanted to continue fighting.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:07.570
In April the crowds were marching under the
banner, "Down with the Provisional Government".

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:10.250
Naturally, Milyukov was forced to resign.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:15.500
The same fate befell to War Minister Guchkov,
who was promptly replaced by the charismatic

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:16.510
Kerensky.

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:21.940
Now a War Minister at the young age of 36,
Kerensky set off for Europe’s Eastern Front.

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:26.180
The former pacifist roused the troops in a
series of emotional speeches, appealing to

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:28.270
them to continue the fighting.

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:32.770
With his new powers, Kerensky appointed legendary
General Brusilov as the Commander in Chief

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:34.039
of the Russian Army.

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:37.270
On June 18th, the War Minister announced a
new offensive.

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:42.690
The July Offensive was an attack on the Austro-Hungarians
in Galician, and initially it was a great

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:43.690
success.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:48.889
But in a repeat of many similar operations
in WWI, low morale and poor supply lines slowed

00:08:48.889 --> 00:08:53.079
down the advance – which was then stopped
completely by German reinforcements.

00:08:53.079 --> 00:08:57.910
Meanwhile, the Bolshevik protests against
the war forced Prince Lvov to resign as Prime

00:08:57.910 --> 00:08:58.910
Minister.

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:03.639
On the 8th of July, he was replaced by the
unstoppable, still-popular Kerensky.

00:09:03.639 --> 00:09:07.860
Ariadna Tyrkova, a member of the Constitutional
Democrat Party, commented:

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:12.749
"Kerensky was perhaps the only member of the
Government who knew how to deal with the masses,

00:09:12.749 --> 00:09:16.559
since he instinctively understood the psychology
of the mob.

00:09:16.559 --> 00:09:20.709
Therein lay his power and the main source
of his popularity"

00:09:20.709 --> 00:09:24.149
Kerensky’s popularity extended outside of
the Russian borders.

00:09:24.149 --> 00:09:27.279
The British and the Americans were great supporters,
too.

00:09:27.279 --> 00:09:31.509
Mansfield Smith-Cumming, head of MI6, arranged
for his Government to receive $1.2 million

00:09:31.509 --> 00:09:34.709
in today’s money, and the Americans sent
a similar gift.

00:09:34.709 --> 00:09:38.910
The Spy master also sent an ‘asset’ to
keep an eye on Kerensky and ensure that he

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:43.630
did not sign an armistice with the Central
Empires -- none other than celebrated author

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:47.429
William Somerset Maugham, who back then was
on the MI6 payroll.

00:09:47.429 --> 00:09:51.179
The novelist wrote, "The long and short of
it was that I should go to Russia and keep

00:09:51.179 --> 00:09:53.680
the Russians in the war."

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.439
Despite Kerensky’s best efforts, the Russian
Army was melting away after the failed offensive.

00:09:58.439 --> 00:10:02.820
Hundreds of thousands deserted, using their
weapons to seize land from the aristocracy

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:04.980
and forcibly redistribute it.

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:09.240
From this moment onward, Kerensky would execute
one bad move after the other, which would

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:39.360
eventually lead to the rise of Lenin and

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:50.980
the Soviets.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:11:49.379
Bad

00:11:49.379 --> 00:11:54.189
move # 1: Spark a military coup
To regain control of the Army, Kerensky fired

00:11:54.189 --> 00:11:58.429
Brusilov and appointed General Kornilov as
Commander-in-Chief.

00:11:58.429 --> 00:12:02.480
Kornilov did not share Kerensky’s social
and democratic views; in fact, he had plans

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:07.119
to restore the death penalty for soldiers
and even to militarize the factories, which

00:12:07.119 --> 00:12:08.810
did not please the Prime Minister.

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:12.149
When Kerensky refused, Kornilov threatened
a military coup.

00:12:12.149 --> 00:12:16.579
He was dismissed before ordering an army contingent
to take control of Petrograd.

00:12:16.579 --> 00:12:17.579
Kerensky was in danger.

00:12:17.579 --> 00:12:21.579
The only ones who could help him were his
uneasy allies, the Bolsheviks with their Red

00:12:21.579 --> 00:12:22.579
Guards.

00:12:22.579 --> 00:12:26.629
Lenin agreed to help, but he made it clear
that they would be fighting against Kornilov,

00:12:26.629 --> 00:12:30.119
and not for Kerensky – an important distinction.

00:12:30.119 --> 00:12:34.290
Bad move # 2: Arm your rivals
The request for help gave the Bolsheviks a

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:38.059
good reason to arm and enlist a force of 25,000
men.

00:12:38.059 --> 00:12:41.619
They fortified Petrograd, ready for a bloody
battle, but it turned out this wasn’t needed.

00:12:41.619 --> 00:12:46.790
A delegation of Red Guards went to meet with
Kornilov's troops, which apparently were more

00:12:46.790 --> 00:12:48.970
chilled than Kerensky expected.

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:53.139
The regular army refused to attack Petrograd
and Kornilov was arrested.

00:12:53.139 --> 00:12:58.920
Bad move # 3: Pick the wrong allies
Kerensky, who by now had a CV as long as the

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:04.699
Volga river, hogged another position: Supreme
Commander of the Russian Army.

00:13:04.699 --> 00:13:09.149
Backed by British and American gold, the leader
continued with the war effort, which dented

00:13:09.149 --> 00:13:10.319
his popularity.

00:13:10.319 --> 00:13:14.329
On the 8th of October, Kerensky attempted
to regain some left-wing support by forming

00:13:14.329 --> 00:13:17.839
a new coalition … but he arguably picked
the wrong allies.

00:13:17.839 --> 00:13:22.920
He invited a majority of Mensheviks and Socialist
Revolutionaries, while the power within the

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.410
left had shifted toward Lenin’s Bolsheviks.

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:31.610
At Kerensky’s own earlier request, the Bolsheviks
now controlled a formidable force of 25,000

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:33.459
armed militiamen.

00:13:33.459 --> 00:13:36.559
Kerensky would not be able to reassert his
authority.

00:13:36.559 --> 00:13:40.309
To be fair to Kerensky, he was in a very difficult
position.

00:13:40.309 --> 00:13:45.759
From the left he was threatened by the Bolsheviks,
hostile to the continuation of the war and

00:13:45.759 --> 00:13:47.489
his support of the Mensheviks.

00:13:47.489 --> 00:13:52.220
From the right, his Provisional Government
could still fall to the schemes of Kornilov,

00:13:52.220 --> 00:13:57.410
who, at least according to Leon Trotsky, could
count on support from old foreign minister

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:58.699
Milyukov.

00:13:58.699 --> 00:14:04.449
On October 31, 1917, Kerensky summoned agent
Somerset Maugham with a desperate request:

00:14:04.449 --> 00:14:08.889
he had to take an urgent secret message to
British Prime Minister David Lloyd George,

00:14:08.889 --> 00:14:10.829
appealing for weapons and ammunition.

00:14:10.829 --> 00:14:14.649
Maugham was unimpressed by Kerensky, describing
a defeated man:

00:14:14.649 --> 00:14:16.639
"His personality had no magnetism.

00:14:16.639 --> 00:14:19.389
He gave no feeling of intellectual or physical
vigour."

00:14:19.389 --> 00:14:22.819
Maugham set off immediately and two days later
he was at 10 Downing Street.

00:14:22.819 --> 00:14:27.120
After the agent told the Prime Minister what
Kerensky wanted, Lloyd George replied:

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:28.250
"I can't do that.

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:30.649
I'm afraid I must bring this conversation
to an end.

00:14:30.649 --> 00:14:33.029
I have a cabinet meeting I must go to."

00:14:33.029 --> 00:14:38.029
In modern political parlance, we could assert
that Kerensky had been totally ghosted by

00:14:38.029 --> 00:14:40.069
his supposed ‘allies.’

00:14:40.069 --> 00:14:44.779
Back in Petrograd, the Bolsheviks had already
started to seize power.

00:14:44.779 --> 00:14:50.439
This was the first week of November for most
of Europe, but to the Russians it was October,

00:14:50.439 --> 00:14:52.339
hence the ‘October Revolution’.

00:14:52.339 --> 00:14:57.790
This is because Russia still used the Julian
calendar, as opposed to the Gregorian calendar,

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:00.319
and lagged 13 days behind.

00:15:00.319 --> 00:15:04.579
On the 7th of November, Kerensky was certain
that the Bolsheviks were about to seize the

00:15:04.579 --> 00:15:06.860
capital and decided to flee.

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:12.529
A story began circulating about Kerensky’s
escape, but there is no confirmation: apparently

00:15:12.529 --> 00:15:17.589
he changed into a women’s dress, either
in a nurse or servant’s uniform, and forced

00:15:17.589 --> 00:15:20.470
his way into a car belonging to the US Embassy.

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:24.869
Because his car had the American flag on it,
Kerensky easily passed all Bolshevik check

00:15:24.869 --> 00:15:25.869
points.

00:15:25.869 --> 00:15:29.799
Later that day, the Red Guards stormed the
Winter Palace and arrested Kerensky's cabinet.

00:15:29.799 --> 00:15:34.149
The only troops loyal to Kerensky were the
Women’s Death Battalion, some young military

00:15:34.149 --> 00:15:36.319
cadets and a detachment of Cossacks.

00:15:36.319 --> 00:15:40.100
After his escape, the deposed leader tried
to get the support of the Russian Army on

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:41.279
the Eastern Front.

00:15:41.279 --> 00:15:45.589
Considering that this was the same Army he
had forced into a failed offensive, resulting

00:15:45.589 --> 00:15:51.499
in mass desertions, they politely declined
his invitation to civil war.

00:15:51.499 --> 00:15:56.179
Kerensky was more successful in rallying loyal
troops from the Northern Front.

00:15:56.179 --> 00:16:00.070
With them, he marched toward Petrograd on
the 13th of November.

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:04.569
On his way to the capital Kerensky led some
Cossack troops into the barracks of Tsarkoye

00:16:04.569 --> 00:16:07.499
Selo, a former Tsarist residence south of
Petrograd.

00:16:07.499 --> 00:16:11.680
John Reed, American journalist and author
of ‘The 10 Days That Shook The World’

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:18.439
described yet another Bad Move:
Bad move # 4: Alienate all military support

00:16:18.439 --> 00:16:21.679
Kerensky entered the town riding a white horse
at the head of his Cossacks.

00:16:21.679 --> 00:16:26.019
At seven in the morning, he sent word to the
local rifle regiment to lay down their arms.

00:16:26.019 --> 00:16:30.139
The soldiers replied they would remain neutral,
but would not disarm.

00:16:30.139 --> 00:16:32.199
Kerensky gave them ten minutes to comply.

00:16:32.199 --> 00:16:36.189
This angered the soldiers, who had been governing
themselves for eight months in an independent

00:16:36.189 --> 00:16:37.189
Soviet.

00:16:37.189 --> 00:16:40.529
A few minutes later, Cossack artillery opened
fire on the barracks, killing eight men.

00:16:40.529 --> 00:16:43.299
Reed wrote:
“From that moment there were no more 'neutral'

00:16:43.299 --> 00:16:45.819
soldiers in Tsarskoye."

00:16:45.819 --> 00:16:49.749
Kerensky could have used a well-trained infantry
regiment for what happened next.

00:16:49.749 --> 00:16:53.860
When Kerensky’s loyalists marched toward
Petrograd, they numbered a mere 600.

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:58.839
On their way, they clashed with 5000 Bolshevik
troops led by Leon Trotsky at the Battle of

00:16:58.839 --> 00:17:03.949
Pulkovo, which could be considered the beginning
of the Russian Civil War.

00:17:03.949 --> 00:17:07.970
Trotsky easily won the engagement, putting
an end to any attempt of armed counter-insurgency

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:13.310
by Kerensky.

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:18.819
Life in Exile
In June 1918 Alexander Kerensky, disguised

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:23.930
as a Serb officer, left Russia, ready to embark
on talks to organize a counter-revolution.

00:17:23.930 --> 00:17:27.990
During the first one and a half years of his
exile, Kerensky lived in Britain, mainly in

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:30.620
the countryside where living costs were lower.

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:35.010
Later, he lived first in Prague, and then
in Paris, where he contributed to various

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:38.530
Russian language newspapers, editing Dni and
Novaia Rossiia.

00:17:38.530 --> 00:17:44.180
While in France, he took part in the inconsequential
in-fighting and intrigues which plagued the

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:45.320
Russian exile community.

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:49.320
Many exiles, nostalgic of Tsarist rule, had
a deep disdain for Kerensky.

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:53.840
Once, a Russian woman, having noticed him
in the street in Paris, said to her child:

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:57.400
‘Look, Tania, here is the man who ruined
Russia’.

00:17:57.400 --> 00:18:01.790
The Russian Civil War between the Red and
White factions was raging, but it was impossible

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:04.800
for Kerensky to provide full support to either
side.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:09.240
While he opposed the Bolsheviks, Kerensky
did not trust the White movement as he considered

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:13.410
its leadership too close to General Kornilov,
his old enemy.

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:19.660
In 1939 Alexander and Olga Kerensky divorced,
and in the same year he married a former Australian

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:21.480
journalist Lidia Tritton.

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:26.640
When the Germans occupied France in June 1940,
Kerensky and Lidia fled to the US, where he

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:29.570
resumed his activities as a journalist and
writer.

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:34.160
When Hitler attacked the Soviet Union in June
1941, Kerensky offered his support to Stalin.

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:38.570
I don’t know what type of answer he was
expecting, but what he got was absolutely

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:39.570
logical.

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:41.660
[Editing note: show image of tumbleweed with
‘swooshing’ wind sound effect]

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:42.660
Ghosted.

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:46.800
Undeterred, Kerensky established radio transmissions
to the USSR to support the military effort.

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:50.910
After the war, he organized the “Union for
Russia’s Liberation,” presumably to restore

00:18:50.910 --> 00:18:56.130
a less extremist regime in Russia, but this
did not achieve any results.

00:18:56.130 --> 00:19:01.330
When his wife Lidia fell terminally ill in
1945, he took her to Brisbane, Australia and

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:05.200
they lived with her family until her death
in February 1946.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:08.800
He then returned to the States, living in
both New York and at Stanford University,

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:11.250
in California, where he taught Russian history.

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:16.550
On April 24, 1970 Kerensky was admitted into
hospital in New York, to recover from a broken

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:19.360
elbow and pelvis sustained in a fall.

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:24.480
At age 89, he did not recover, and on the
11th of June he died of heart disease, or

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:26.320
cancer, depending on the sources.

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:30.260
Even after his dying moments, this was a man
who would elicit strong reactions.

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:33.470
According to his grandson, there were some
nurses in the hospital staff of Russian origin

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:38.470
and they refused to touch him, because they
held him responsible for starting the revolution.

00:19:38.470 --> 00:19:41.950
The local Russian Orthodox Church refused
to bury him as they considered him to be a

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:45.130
freemason, and the man behind Tsarist Russia’s
fall.

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:47.990
The local Serb Orthodox Church also refused
to bury him.

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:52.250
His body was moved to London and buried at
Putney Vale cemetery, London, where he had

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:57.510
spent the very first part of his exile and
where his sons lived.

00:19:57.510 --> 00:20:04.520
Legacy
For 12 years, Alexander Kerensky had been

00:20:04.520 --> 00:20:09.220
a skilled lawyer, a brilliant orator, and
a well-rounded politician who at a very young

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:11.870
age had covered many important posts.

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:16.500
His moderate democratic socialism, while calling
for the abolition of the Tsar’s rule, was

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:20.250
a needed balancing element for the Bolshevik
excesses.

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:25.490
And yet, because of what he did in only four
months – from July to October 1917 -- he

00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:31.480
would be shunned by revolutionaries and reactionaries
alike, mocked for his supposedly Napoleonic

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:34.770
ambitions and generally portrayed as a failure.

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:40.180
Some historians even believe that rather than
fearing a military coup by his enemy Kornilov,

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:44.870
Kerensky was actually manipulating the General
to create his own dictatorship as a countermeasure.

00:20:44.870 --> 00:20:49.790
His limited inquiries into Kornilov’ fail
bid sparked suspicions that he may have had

00:20:49.790 --> 00:20:51.700
authoritarian intentions.

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:55.700
Perhaps the reason for Kerensky’s failure
in Government was best explained by British

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:58.980
politician and journalist Morgan Philips Price.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:02.730
In the Manchester Guardian of 19th November
1917, he wrote:

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:06.900
“The Government of Kerensky fell before
the Bolshevik insurgents because it had no

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:08.530
supporters in the country.

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:13.010
The bourgeois parties and the generals and
the staff disliked it because it would not

00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:15.020
establish a military dictatorship.

00:21:15.020 --> 00:21:19.050
The Revolutionary Democracy lost faith in
it because after eight months it had neither

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:24.570
given land to the peasants nor established
State control of industries, nor advanced

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:29.100
the cause of the Russian peace programme”
In other words: Kerensky was caught between

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:35.750
two opposing factions that were held together
only by a common enemy: the rule of Tsar Nicholas

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:36.750
II.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:41.660
In an attempt to keep them together, Kerensky’s
bad moves had alienated all potential support,

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:46.120
while arming his most motivated rivals Lenin
and Trotsky.

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:50.170
The February Revolution led to the October
Revolution, which led to the Soviet Union,

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:55.820
the longest running totalitarian state in
Europe – and to the Cold War.

00:21:55.820 --> 00:22:00.170
It begs the question: what could have Russia
and the rest of the world been, had Kerensky

00:22:00.170 --> 00:22:05.520
made a few different choices?

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:22.551
That’s all 
for today, please like and subscribe and usual

00:22:22.551 --> 00:22:23.050
… thank you for watching!

